## Input

Optional free-form text after the command:

- **No extra text** — survey candidates and ask which to pick.
- **`fix`** — skip the survey and create a single new "general fixes" GitHub issue (small bug / typo / chore bucket), then work on it. A new issue is created **every time** `fix` is used.
- **`label:<L>`** — **hard filter**: shortlist only open issues carrying GitHub label `<L>` (case-insensitive; strip whitespace after the colon). Compatible with `noplan` / `root:` / `repo:`. Empty after filter → stop (“no open issues with label `<L>`”). Never auto-pick even when only one match. Batch equivalent: `/iflow-cycle label:<L>` (or `/iflow-cycle yolo` for the configured yolo trigger).
- **A hint** (e.g. `milestone v0.4`, `something about templating`, `the docs ones`) — soft-bias the candidate ranking in Phase 1 when `label:<L>` is **absent**. Free-form text that names a label is **not** a hard filter — use the `label:` token.
- **`noplan`** — skip the `auto_plan` chain for this run (ask before `/iflow-plan` even when baked `auto_plan` is true).

## Phases

`/iflow-pick` runs in three phases. Stop and ask whenever a choice is ambiguous; never create a GitHub issue or branch without explicit confirmation.

### Phase 1 — choose the issue

0. **Preflight.** Detect the default branch (`gh repo view --json defaultBranchRef -q .defaultBranchRef.name`; fall back to `git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD | sed 's|^origin/||'`, else `main`). Run `git fetch --prune`. Report the current branch and whether the working tree is clean (`git status --porcelain`).

1. **`fix` shortcut.** If the user passed `fix`, skip candidate selection: go straight to creating a new general-fixes issue (see step 4) and continue to Phase 2.

2. **Source the candidates** (in precedence order):
   - **Parse `label:<L>`** when present (case-insensitive label name after the colon). Announce the active filter in the shortlist header.
   - **Parked work first.** If `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ partly_solved_folder }}/` contains any `issue<n>_*` groups, list them as the **primary** candidates — these are already-started issues that deserve to be finished before new work begins. Show issue number, title (from each `issue<n>_original.md`), and a one-line status if a status file exists. When `label:<L>` is active, keep only parked issues that carry `<L>` (`gh issue view <n> --json labels` when unclear).
   - **Active epic next.** If an epic plan under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ epics_folder }}/` has open issues (or the user named one, e.g. `epic 144`), prefer its **current stage's unblocked issues**. Fast path: `issue-flow agent epic-status <N> --json` — its `next_candidates` are exactly the open, dependency-satisfied issues of the current stage. Surface those near the top so the epic advances stage by stage instead of stalling. When `label:<L>` is active, keep only epic candidates that carry `<L>`.
   - **Otherwise, pull from GitHub.** Run `gh issue list --state open --json number,title,labels,milestone,updatedAt` (add `--repo owner/repo` if the default remote is ambiguous). When `label:<L>` is active, add `--label <L>` (hard filter). Drop any issue already captured locally under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}/`, `{{ partly_solved_folder }}/`, or `{{ solved_folder }}/`. If the filtered set is empty, **stop** with “no open issues with label `<L>`.”

3. **Rank by relevance and present a shortlist.** Rank candidates using, in combination:
   - **Epic membership** — an active epic's current-stage `next_candidates` first (note the epic + stage on those entries).
   - **Milestone** — prefer issues on the nearest / active milestone (and honour any milestone hint in the input).
   - **Labels** — prefer labels that match recent work or any soft label/topic hint when no `label:` filter is active.
   - **Topical similarity** — bias toward issues whose title/labels resemble recently solved issues (skim `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ solved_folder }}/` and recent branch names) so related work clusters together.

   Present a short ranked shortlist (roughly 3–7 entries) as a numbered list with number, title, labels, and milestone, then **ask the user to confirm** the top pick or choose another. Always allow a free-form override (a different issue number, URL, or `fix`). Do not silently pick one — even when the filtered shortlist has a single entry.

4. **Create a `fix` issue (only when requested).** When the user passed `fix` (or explicitly asks for a general-fixes bucket): create a **new** GitHub issue with `gh issue create` (e.g. title `chore: general fixes`, body noting it is a catch-all for small fixes/typos/chores). Confirm the title/body with the user first. Record the returned issue number and use it as the chosen issue. A fresh issue is created each time — existing open general-fixes issues are **not** reused.

5. **Over-large issue (offer only).** If the chosen issue looks too involved to land in one PR, **mention** `/iflow-split` (flat parent/child) or `/iflow-epic` (staged) and ask. Default is proceed with the whole issue. Do **not** create children here.
{% if label_flows and "iflow-yolo" in included_commands %}
6. **Label-driven yolo flow.** If the chosen issue carries the **`{{ yolo_label }}`** label (case-insensitive; check the `labels` field from `gh issue list`, or `gh issue view <n> --json labels` when the issue was given directly), the issue is marked for the hands-off `/iflow-yolo` chain:
   - Announce it: "issue #<N> has the `{{ yolo_label }}` label → running it through `/iflow-yolo`".
   - Fold `/iflow-yolo`'s single consolidated confirm into the pick confirmation — one prompt total, listing the branch to create and the full chain (`init → plan → build → close yolo`). Require an explicit yes; any other answer falls back to the normal (non-yolo) flow or aborts, per the user's answer.
   - On yes: run Phase 2 (create the branch) as usual, then follow `{{ agent_dir }}/commands/iflow-yolo.md` **instead of** the plain Phase 3 handoff. The yolo preflight (clean tree, passing tests) still runs and can still abort; do not re-ask its consolidated confirm — the combined prompt above already covered it.
   - This routing is configurable: `label_flows = false` under `[issueflow]` in `{{ issueflows_dir }}/config.toml` turns it off, and `yolo_label` renames the trigger label (re-run `issue-flow update` after changing either).
{% endif %}
{% if model_label_flows %}
7. **Label-driven model profile.** If the chosen issue carries the **`{{ deep_model_label }}`** label (case-insensitive), announce that the session should use the **reasoning** profile for all steps. If it carries **`{{ fast_model_label }}`**, announce **economy**. When both match, **reasoning** wins. Configurable via `model_label_flows`, `deep_model_label`, and `fast_model_label` under `[issueflow]` in `{{ issueflows_dir }}/config.toml` (re-run `issue-flow update` after changing).
{% endif %}

### Phase 2 — create the branch

1. **Working-tree gate.** Prefer `issue-flow agent preflight --json`
   (`clean` / `dirty_paths` / `issueflows_only`); else `git status --porcelain`
   and treat the tree as issueflows-only when every path is under
   `{{ issueflows_dir }}/`.
   - **Clean** — continue.
   - **Issueflows-only dirty** (e.g. leftover `/iflow-doctor` moves) — one
     consolidated prompt; **recommended default** = commit housekeeping on the
     **current** branch (`chore: doctor housekeeping — archive/sweep
     {{ issueflows_dir }} groups`, or a short edit), then continue. Alternatives:
     stash / abort. Stage **only** those paths; **no push**. Do not branch on
     top of the dirty tree.
   - **Mixed / code dirty** — **stop**; list paths outside
     `{{ issueflows_dir }}/`; ask commit / stash / abort. Do not offer “commit
     everything”.

2. **Branch off the default.** Switch to the default branch and fast-forward if needed, then create the issue branch using GitHub's numeric convention `git switch -c <N>-<short-slug>` (leading issue number, then a short kebab-case slug derived from the title). Confirm the branch name with the user if the slug is non-obvious.

3. **Run `/iflow-init` automatically.** The issue number is now known, so follow the `{{ agent_dir }}/commands/iflow-init.md` playbook for `<N>` (fetch the issue + comments, write `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}/issue<N>_original.md`, run its archive sweep). Do not re-implement that logic here — delegate to it.

### Phase 3 — hand off to the standard flow

{% if auto_plan and "iflow_plan" in included_skills %}
1. The issue is now captured on a fresh branch. This project has `auto_plan = true` — **follow** `{{ agent_dir }}/{{ commands_dir }}/iflow-plan.md` (or the `iflow-plan` skill) immediately and note the chain. Trailing **`noplan`** skips once and falls back to asking.
{% else %}
1. The issue is now captured on a fresh branch. **Ask the user whether to continue with `/iflow-plan`** next (the usual next step). Do **not** auto-run it — make the handoff explicit so the user stays in control.
{% endif %}
{% if label_flows and "iflow-yolo" in included_commands %}
2. **Exception:** when Phase 1 step 6 confirmed the `{{ yolo_label }}`-label yolo routing, skip this handoff — the `/iflow-yolo` chain (which includes its own `/iflow-init`) takes over after the branch is created.
{% endif %}

## Constraints

- **Off-path.** Never auto-dispatch `/iflow-pick` from `/iflow`, `/iflow-build`, or `/iflow-close`. The user opts in.
- **Never create GitHub issues or branches without explicit confirmation.** Show what will be created first.
- **Respect git safety norms.** Branch off the detected default, never force-push, never delete branches from this command.
- Over-large splits are **offer-only**: never create child issues from this command. Point at `/iflow-split` (flat) or `/iflow-epic` (staged).
- Delegate issue capture to `/iflow-init` rather than duplicating its fetch/archive logic.

## Output to user

Report:
- which source the candidate came from (parked work vs GitHub) and the ranked shortlist shown
- the chosen issue number and title (and, for `fix`, that a new general-fixes issue was created)
- the branch created (`<N>-<slug>`) and that `/iflow-init` ran (file path written)
- the handoff prompt result (whether the user wants `/iflow-plan` next)
{% if label_flows and "iflow-yolo" in included_commands %}
- whether the `{{ yolo_label }}` label routed the issue into `/iflow-yolo` (and, if so, defer the rest of the report to that command's output)
{% endif %}

## Example invocations

- `/iflow-pick` — survey parked + GitHub issues, show a ranked shortlist, confirm, branch, init, hand off.
- `/iflow-pick fix` — create a new `chore: general fixes` issue, branch, init, hand off.
- `/iflow-pick label:enhancement` — hard-filter the shortlist to open issues with the `enhancement` label, then confirm / branch / init.
- `/iflow-pick milestone v0.4` — soft-bias the ranking toward the `v0.4` milestone before asking.
- Batch the same label filter (hands-off): `/iflow-cycle label:enhancement` (or `/iflow-cycle yolo` for the configured yolo trigger).
